This is false. Let
$$
K=\mathbb Q,qquad L=\mathbb Q(\sqrt[3]2),qquad
M=\mathbb Q(\sqrt[3]2,\omega),
$$
where $\omega$ is a primitive cube root of unity. Then $M$ is the <splitting field> of $T^3-2$ over $\mathbb Q$, so $M/K$ is normal. The <irreducible polynomial> $T^3-2$ has a root in $L$ but does not split there, so $L/K$ is not normal.

